    "transcript": [
      {
        "role": "H1",
        "text": "Phillips-Curve Expectations",
        "node": 0
      },
      {
        "role": "P",
        "text": "MACROECONOMICS",
        "node": 1
      },
      {
        "role": "P",
        "text": "MACRO-39",
        "node": 2
      },
      {
        "role": "P",
        "text": "Time: 5 minutes",
        "node": 3
      },
      {
        "role": "P",
        "text": "Outcome: Analyze expected inflation and supply shocks as Phillips-curve shifters.",
        "node": 4
      },
      {
        "role": "P",
        "text": "Difficulty: Intermediate",
        "node": 5
      },
      {
        "role": "H2",
        "text": "THE CORE IDEA",
        "node": 6
      },
      {
        "role": "P",
        "text": "Expected inflation and supply shocks shift the short-run Phillips curve rather than moving the economy along one curve. Higher expected inflation raises actual inflation at each unemployment rate and shifts the curve upward; lower expectations shift it downward. Adverse supply shocks can also raise inflation at a given unemployment rate. A demand change, by contrast, moves the economy along the current short-run curve while expectations remain fixed.",
        "node": 7
      },
      {
        "role": "H2",
        "text": "HOW TO RECOGNIZE IT",
        "node": 8
      },
      {
        "role": "Lbl",
        "text": "•",
        "node": 11
      },
      {
        "role": "LBody",
        "text": "A movement along one short-run Phillips curve reflects a change in aggregate demand with expected inflation fixed.",
        "node": 12
      },
      {
        "role": "Lbl",
        "text": "•",
        "node": 14
      },
      {
        "role": "LBody",
        "text": "Higher expected inflation shifts the short-run Phillips curve upward; lower expected inflation shifts it downward.",
        "node": 15
      },
      {
        "role": "Lbl",
        "text": "•",
        "node": 17
      },
      {
        "role": "LBody",
        "text": "An adverse supply shock can shift the curve upward by raising inflation at each unemployment rate.",
        "node": 18
      },
      {
        "role": "Lbl",
        "text": "•",
        "node": 20
      },
      {
        "role": "LBody",
        "text": "Compare inflation at the same unemployment rate to identify a shift rather than a movement along the curve.",
        "node": 21
      },
      {
        "role": "H2",
        "text": "WATCH OUT",
        "node": 22
      },
      {
        "role": "P",
        "text": "Do not confuse a curve shift with movement along one curve. At the same unemployment rate, a higher SRPC reflects higher expected inflation or a worse tradeoff.",
        "node": 23
      },
      {
        "role": "H2",
        "text": "WORKED EXAMPLE: SHIFTING THE PHILLIPS CURVE",
        "node": 24
      },
      {
        "role": "Figure",
        "text": "Horizontal axis: unemployment rate; vertical axis: inflation rate. A vertical LRPC stands at U2. SRPC1 is the lower, leftward downward-sloping curve; SRPC2 is the higher, rightward downward-sloping curve. Point a on SRPC1 is at unemployment U1 and inflation I2. Point b, where SRPC2 meets LRPC, is at U2 and I2. Point c, where SRPC1 meets LRPC, is at U2 and I1. Point d on SRPC2 is at U3 and I1. From left to right the unemployment labels are U1, U2, U3; I2 is above I1. Dashed guides identify all four points. No numerical rates are printed.",
        "node": 25
      },
      {
        "role": "P",
        "text": "The economy begins at point b on SRPC2 with unemployment U2 and inflation I2. When expected inflation falls, the short-run Phillips curve shifts downward to SRPC1. At the natural unemployment rate U2, the economy reaches point c with lower inflation I1. Expectations, not a permanent unemployment change, drive the shift.",
        "node": 26
      },
      {
        "role": "H2",
        "text": "CHECK YOURSELF",
        "node": 27
      },
      {
        "role": "P",
        "text": "With unemployment held at U2, expected inflation falls. Does the economy move along SRPC2 or shift to SRPC1, and what happens to inflation?",
        "node": 28
      },
      {
        "role": "H2",
        "text": "READY?",
        "node": 29
      },
      {
        "role": "P",
        "text": "Return to the game and master this concept.",
        "node": 30
      }
    ],
